Give the extension host Worker its own memory budget instead of inheriting the main thread's --max-old-space-size.

NodeRuntimeController.swift starts the main process with
--max-old-space-size=256 (a deliberate, mobile-conscious budget for the
main thread's own footprint). extensionHostConnection.ts's iOS
worker_threads branch (ios-exthost-no-fork.diff) builds its Worker's own
execArgv/resourceLimits from `opts.execArgv`, which is just
`process.execArgv` (the main process's own flags) minus --inspect --
meaning that same 256MB ceiling was being silently re-applied to the
extension host's own, separate V8 isolate, not just stripped like the
jitless/wasm flags already are.

Confirmed as a real, deterministic bug via a captured node-stdio.log, not
guessed: the extension host Worker OOMs and gets torn down and restarted
roughly 15-20 seconds after every single launch --

  Error [ERR_WORKER_OUT_OF_MEMORY]: Worker terminated due to reaching
  memory limit: JS heap out of memory

-- reproducing identically in both the pre- and post-UI-test app launches
of the same simulator-test.yml run. 256MB is far too little for vscode's
extension host, which ordinarily runs unconstrained (a real forked OS
process inheriting the platform's own multi-GB V8 default) on desktop.
This is not a real device memory-pressure issue either -- it reproduces
in the Simulator, which has ample host RAM available -- purely a
self-imposed V8 old-space ceiling, unrelated to iOS's actual jetsam
memory limit (a separate, much higher ceiling this change doesn't touch).
A reconnect-and-relaunch cycle already built into vscode's own
client/server protocol silently recovers (a second Worker takes over),
which is why this had gone unnoticed: the UI still renders correctly a
few seconds later, no visible crash, just a slower, wasteful cold start
on every single boot.

Fix: give the extension host Worker its own explicit resourceLimits
(512MB), independent of whatever --max-old-space-size the main thread
happens to be running with.

